To find a root of the equation $f(x) = 0$, start with an initial guess $x_0$ and generate a sequence of approximations using:

\[
\boxed{x_{n+1} = x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}}
\]

for $n = 0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots$

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{Geometric Interpretation:}}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Start at point $(x_n, f(x_n))$ on the curve
    \item Draw the tangent line at this point
    \item Find where the tangent line crosses the $x$-axis
    \item This $x$-intercept becomes $x_{n+1}$ (next approximation)
    \item Repeat the process
\end{enumerate}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{The Formula Derivation:}}

The tangent line at $(x_n, f(x_n))$ has equation:
\[
y - f(x_n) = f'(x_n)(x - x_n)
\]

Setting $y = 0$ (to find $x$-intercept):
\begin{align*}
-f(x_n) &= f'(x_n)(x - x_n) \\
x - x_n &= -\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} \\
x &= x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}
\end{align*}

This gives us $x_{n+1}$.

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Requirements:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f'(x_n) \neq 0$ (tangent line not horizontal)
    \item Good initial guess $x_0$ (close to actual root)
    \item Function should be "well-behaved" near the root
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Use Newton-Raphson method to approximate $\sqrt{2}$ (i.e., solve $x^2 - 2 = 0$) starting with $x_0 = 1$. Find three iterations.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Set up the function and its derivative

\begin{align*}
f(x) &= x^2 - 2 \\
f'(x) &= 2x
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Write the iteration formula

\begin{align*}
x_{n+1} &= x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} \\
&= x_n - \frac{x_n^2 - 2}{2x_n} \\
&= x_n - \frac{x_n}{2} + \frac{1}{x_n} \\
&= \frac{x_n}{2} + \frac{1}{x_n} \\
&= \frac{x_n^2 + 2}{2x_n}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Perform iterations

\textbf{Iteration 0:} $x_0 = 1$ (given)

\textbf{Iteration 1:}
\begin{align*}
x_1 &= \frac{x_0^2 + 2}{2x_0} = \frac{1 + 2}{2(1)} = \frac{3}{2} = 1.5
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 2:}
\begin{align*}
x_2 &= \frac{x_1^2 + 2}{2x_1} = \frac{(1.5)^2 + 2}{2(1.5)} = \frac{2.25 + 2}{3} = \frac{4.25}{3} \approx 1.41667
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 3:}
\begin{align*}
x_3 &= \frac{x_2^2 + 2}{2x_2} = \frac{(1.41667)^2 + 2}{2(1.41667)} \\
&\approx \frac{2.00694 + 2}{2.83334} \approx \frac{4.00694}{2.83334} \approx 1.41421
\end{align*}

\textbf{Answer:} 
\begin{align*}
x_1 &= \boxed{1.5} \\
x_2 &= \boxed{1.41667} \\
x_3 &= \boxed{1.41421}
\end{align*}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} $\sqrt{2} = 1.41421356...$, so $x_3$ is accurate to 5 decimal places after just 3 iterations!

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{Observation:}} The method converges very quickly - this is called "quadratic convergence."

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} Use Newton-Raphson method to approximate a root of $e^x - 3x = 0$ up to four decimal places, starting with $x_0 = 0$.

(From 2024 past paper, Q3 part a)

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Set up the function and derivative

\begin{align*}
f(x) &= e^x - 3x \\
f'(x) &= e^x - 3
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Write iteration formula

\begin{align*}
x_{n+1} &= x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} \\
&= x_n - \frac{e^{x_n} - 3x_n}{e^{x_n} - 3}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3:} Perform iterations starting with $x_0 = 0$

\textbf{Iteration 0:} $x_0 = 0$

\textbf{Iteration 1:}
\begin{align*}
f(0) &= e^0 - 3(0) = 1 - 0 = 1 \\
f'(0) &= e^0 - 3 = 1 - 3 = -2 \\
x_1 &= 0 - \frac{1}{-2} = 0 - (-0.5) = 0.5
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 2:}
\begin{align*}
f(0.5) &= e^{0.5} - 3(0.5) = 1.6487 - 1.5 = 0.1487 \\
f'(0.5) &= e^{0.5} - 3 = 1.6487 - 3 = -1.3513 \\
x_2 &= 0.5 - \frac{0.1487}{-1.3513} = 0.5 + 0.1100 = 0.6100
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 3:}
\begin{align*}
f(0.6100) &= e^{0.61} - 3(0.61) = 1.8404 - 1.83 = 0.0104 \\
f'(0.6100) &= e^{0.61} - 3 = 1.8404 - 3 = -1.1596 \\
x_3 &= 0.61 - \frac{0.0104}{-1.1596} = 0.61 + 0.0090 = 0.6190
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 4:}
\begin{align*}
f(0.6190) &= e^{0.619} - 3(0.619) = 1.8572 - 1.857 = 0.0002 \\
f'(0.6190) &= e^{0.619} - 3 = 1.8572 - 3 = -1.1428 \\
x_4 &= 0.619 - \frac{0.0002}{-1.1428} = 0.619 + 0.0002 = 0.6192
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 5:}
\begin{align*}
x_5 &\approx 0.6192
\end{align*}

Since $x_4$ and $x_5$ agree to 4 decimal places, we stop.

\textbf{Answer:} The root is approximately \boxed{x \approx 0.6192} (to 4 decimal places)

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Verification:}} $f(0.6192) = e^{0.6192} - 3(0.6192) \approx 0.000003 \approx 0$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} The equation $x^3 - 2x - 5 = 0$ has one real root. Use Newton-Raphson method with $x_0 = 2$ to approximate it to 4 decimal places.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Set up the functions

\begin{align*}
f(x) &= x^3 - 2x - 5 \\
f'(x) &= 3x^2 - 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Iteration formula

\[
x_{n+1} = x_n - \frac{x_n^3 - 2x_n - 5}{3x_n^2 - 2}
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Perform iterations

\textbf{$x_0 = 2$:}

\textbf{Iteration 1:}
\begin{align*}
f(2) &= 8 - 4 - 5 = -1 \\
f'(2) &= 12 - 2 = 10 \\
x_1 &= 2 - \frac{-1}{10} = 2.1
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 2:}
\begin{align*}
f(2.1) &= (2.1)^3 - 2(2.1) - 5 = 9.261 - 4.2 - 5 = 0.061 \\
f'(2.1) &= 3(2.1)^2 - 2 = 13.23 - 2 = 11.23 \\
x_2 &= 2.1 - \frac{0.061}{11.23} = 2.1 - 0.0054 = 2.0946
\end{align*}

\textbf{Iteration 3:}
\begin{align*}
f(2.0946) &\approx 0.0003 \\
f'(2.0946) &\approx 11.1575 \\
x_3 &= 2.0946 - \frac{0.0003}{11.1575} \approx 2.0946
\end{align*}

Convergence achieved!

\textbf{Answer:} \boxed{x \approx 2.0946}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Check:}} $f(2.0946) = (2.0946)^3 - 2(2.0946) - 5 \approx 0.0001 \approx 0$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.5cm}

The Newton-Raphson method may fail or converge slowly when:

\textbf{1. Poor Initial Guess:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $x_0$ is far from the actual root
    \item May converge to a different root
    \item May oscillate or diverge
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. $f'(x_n) = 0$ or very small:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Division by zero or near-zero
    \item Tangent line nearly horizontal
    \item Next iterate may jump very far
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. Multiple Roots:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item At multiple roots, $f'(x) = 0$
    \item Convergence is slower (linear instead of quadratic)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. Cycles:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Iterations may cycle between values
    \item Never converge to a root
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faInfoCircle\ \textbf{How to Improve:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Choose better initial guess (graph the function first)
    \item Use a different method if Newton-Raphson fails
    \item Check for convergence (iterations should get closer)
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Problem:}} For $f(x) = x^2 - 4$, starting with $x_0 = 1$, show the first three Newton-Raphson iterations and discuss convergence.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1:} Set up

\begin{align*}
f(x) &= x^2 - 4 \\
f'(x) &= 2x \\
x_{n+1} &= x_n - \frac{x_n^2 - 4}{2x_n} = \frac{x_n^2 + 4}{2x_n}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2:} Iterations

\textbf{$x_0 = 1$:}
\[
x_1 = \frac{1 + 4}{2} = 2.5
\]

\textbf{$x_1 = 2.5$:}
\[
x_2 = \frac{6.25 + 4}{5} = \frac{10.25}{5} = 2.05
\]

\textbf{$x_2 = 2.05$:}
\[
x_3 = \frac{(2.05)^2 + 4}{2(2.05)} = \frac{4.2025 + 4}{4.1} = \frac{8.2025}{4.1} \approx 2.0006
\]

\textbf{Step 3:} Analysis

The sequence: $1 \to 2.5 \to 2.05 \to 2.0006 \to ...$

Converging to $x = 2$ (since $f(2) = 4 - 4 = 0$).

\textbf{Convergence table:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Iteration} & \textbf{$x_n$} & \textbf{Error} $|x_n - 2|$ \\
\hline
0 & 1.0000 & 1.0000 \\
\hline
1 & 2.5000 & 0.5000 \\
\hline
2 & 2.0500 & 0.0500 \\
\hline
3 & 2.0006 & 0.0006 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Answer:} The method converges \textbf{rapidly} (quadratically) to the root $x = 2$.

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Note:}} Each iteration roughly squares the error, which is characteristic of quadratic convergence.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Section 3.4 Summary: Newton-Raphson Method}}

\textbf{The Formula:}
\[
\boxed{x_{n+1} = x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}}
\]

\textbf{Algorithm Steps:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Choose initial guess $x_0$ (close to actual root)
    \item Compute $f(x_n)$ and $f'(x_n)$
    \item Calculate $x_{n+1} = x_n - \dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}$
    \item Repeat until desired accuracy achieved
    \item Stop when $|x_{n+1} - x_n| < \epsilon$ (tolerance)
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Advantages:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Very fast convergence (quadratic)
    \item Works for many types of equations
    \item Simple to implement
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Disadvantages:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Requires derivative
    \item May not converge with poor initial guess
    \item Fails when $f'(x_n) = 0$
    \item May converge to wrong root
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\faExclamationTriangle\ \textbf{Important Tips:}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Always check convergence (iterations getting closer)
    \item Graph the function to find good starting point
    \item Stop when successive iterates agree to desired decimal places
    \item Be careful with rounding during calculations
    \item Verify your answer by substituting into original equation
\end{itemize}

\textcolor{green}{\textbf{Exam Pattern:}}

Past papers typically ask for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item 3-5 iterations
    \item Accuracy to 4 decimal places
    \item Show all working clearly
    \item State the iteration formula first
\end{itemize}

